WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.040
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.560
The continued comedy of errors that also has
a major humanitarian death toll.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.560
Right?

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.250
So we don't want to merely call it a comedy
of errors.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:17.640
Russia has apparently shot down their own
$36 million bomber jet an airplane.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.970
This is according to Ukraine.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.980
There's a Newsweek article explaining it.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:27.900
A $36 million Russian sue, 34 bomber was reportedly
shot down by Russia's own forces over Ukraine's

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.990
eastern Luhansk region, according to Ukrainian
media outlets.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:36.020
Yesterday, the jet was reportedly down near
Olszewski, a city in the Luhansk region.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.130
This is where the war is currently focused
and Olszewski is currently under the occupation

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:44.020
of Moscow backed separatist forces.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.420
A video and images shared by the Strategic
Command of the Armed Forces of Ukraine on

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:55.750
the Telegram messaging app appear to show
the charred remains of a Russian SU 34 fighter

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:56.750
bomber.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.950
Now, there is there's some confusion about
this because there were reports that the picture

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:06.960
of a non destroyed bomber is not actually
of the se you 34.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.270
And there's some debate as to whether the
reporting is showing the right planes or whether

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.970
the reporting is describing the right planes.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:21.830
It appears to be that it is an SU 34, but
that some of the images are not actually of

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:26.390
that plane, which makes you wonder, you know,
it makes you wonder a number of different

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:32.880
things, although I believe that this picture
that I'm showing you is of the S2 34.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.260
You know, these types of accidents actually
do happen in in war.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.780
It's not the strangest thing.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.360
And when you get over 100 days, right.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:48.830
I mean, we're into month four at this point
of a war where neither side is dramatically

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:53.820
well-organized when it comes to air warfare.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:54.820
There are.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:58.509
There's similar looking equipment on both
sides.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:00.690
It's not the most unheard of thing.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:07.150
But at the same time, we don't currently know
exactly how this plane was shot down.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:08.939
Was it shot down by ground fire?

00:02:08.939 --> 00:02:14.489
You would think that any air defense missiles
would be checking for identifiers where planes

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:15.680
have an identifier.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.159
I guess if the identifier system malfunctioned
or if it didn't work, maybe Russian troops

00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:25.689
thought this was an enemy bomber and they
shot it down as a result.

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:29.620
But it's a good opportunity to kind of zoom
out and talk about what's going on in Ukraine.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.090
Bigger picture and it's not good.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:38.510
And I've I've spoken with you before about
how the stories that make good stories for

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:45.340
corporate media are stories that have a relatively
clearly defined beginning, middle and end.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.950
And if you think back to coverage of the Iraq
war in 2003 and the lead up you had making

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:56.659
the case for the war, that was a clear like
a pre war phase.

00:02:56.659 --> 00:03:02.470
And that included the testimony before Congress,
which included faulty intelligence, as we

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:03.470
now know.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.379
It included George W Bush making the case
and ultimately George W Bush announcing the

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:08.379
start of the war.

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:14.099
And then it went to missiles and the entire
thing, the actual meat of the war coverage.

00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:16.310
And then you had the Mission Accomplished
banner.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:23.000
And even though after that is when most of
the civilian and military casualties took

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.819
place, after the Mission Accomplished moment,
there was less coverage of the war as an event

00:03:28.819 --> 00:03:29.819
that is ongoing.

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:32.040
It became background at that point.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.430
And this is why and I know many of you have
heard this already, but I think it's important

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:39.870
to understand and for me to explain how a
lot of these corporate media narratives kind

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.279
of work.

00:03:41.279 --> 00:03:46.920
If you have a story like Hunger Deaths among
African children, which is sort of like your

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:54.010
prototypical tragedy in the sense that it's
always going on and there's a question of,

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.409
well, when and how is it covered?

00:03:56.409 --> 00:03:57.870
It's ongoing.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:05.449
And this is why the constant deaths of children
from hunger in Africa don't get the attention

00:04:05.449 --> 00:04:10.859
in Western media, as one or two people are
killed in a school, shooting as an example

00:04:10.859 --> 00:04:14.459
doesn't mean any of the deaths are any more
or less tragic.

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:17.880
And in terms of prevent ability, that that's
a whole other conversation to have as well.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.700
But with the invasion of Ukraine by Russia,
we are now.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:23.920
Kind of in that middle phase.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.580
And that middle phase can be very, very long.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.530
And at this point, you can make the case that
nothing new is happening now.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:35.060
Every time bombs are dropped, every time an
apartment building is bombed, every time people

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.120
are killed, those are new things that are
happening.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.780
It's just as tragic.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:42.590
One person, 101,000 is killed as when it's
person number one.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:46.900
But the stories start to blend and people
start to say, well, weren't they already in

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.110
the Luhansk region?

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.880
Was this the Donetsk or what was it?

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.980
I think the the the infirmary or the nursery
was already bombed, was, oh, this one's in

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:57.020
a different place.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.140
And so the coverage we are seeing in corporate
media starts to reflect that prototypical,

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:06.880
prototypical for independent shows like ours,
the challenge becomes how to continue covering

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:13.530
it, given that the resources of shows like
ours to have people on the ground are dramatically

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:14.530
more limited.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:20.040
And so to a certain degree, we are reacting
to the coverage that is taking place, which

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.010
is certainly diminished at this point.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:28.120
But the latest news is that that this is sort
of the individual element of the story.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.160
Russia apparently shooting down one of its
own fighter jets.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:35.641
And, you know, we could we could call it a
comedy of errors and stop there if it weren't

00:05:35.641 --> 00:05:35.651
so tragic.

